  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1041 PM CDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nThe latest 500mb analysis indicates a broad 596 dam ridge\ncentered over the ArkLaTex region with us here in the Mid-South\nsituated along its northeastern quadrant. This is good news as\nover the past several days, this ridge has been centered over us\nwith heights around 597-599 dam. However, dangerous heat will\nstill be witnessed across the entire Mid-South tomorrow with\nareas mainly west of Jackson Tennessee and south of the Tennessee\nand Mississippi state line seeing heat indices 110+F. These areas\nremain under an Extreme Heat Warning through Monday with the rest\nof the Mid-South now under a Heat Advisory for heat indices\n105+F. Please take heat safety precautions seriously as we are\nseveral days into this heatwave.\n\nOvernight Monday, a weak cold frontal boundary, currently\nextending across the southern Ohio River Valley and into\nsoutheastern Missouri, will begin to dip down into our area.\nAhead of it, moisture pooling is anticipated. CAMs continue to\nhint at isolated shower and thunderstorm development Monday\nafternoon as dewpoints in the low to mid 70s could have the\npotential to filter in. However, increased dewpoints may very\nwell mix out by the mid afternoon hours, potentially cutting off\noverall shower and thunderstorm activity. Any areas that do see\nconvective development will also see brief relief from oppressive\nheat Monday afternoon.\n\nThe persistent ridge will continue to retrograde allowing 500mb\nheights to gradually fall, heading into Tuesday as quasi-zonal\nflow begins to filter in aloft. Many areas, especially over\nnorthern Mississippi and in eastern Arkansas will likely continue\nto see heat indices 105+F Tuesday with continued influence from\nupper-level ridging. By Wednesday, as pretty strong return flow\nmoves over the region, we could see yet another triple digit air\ntemperature day with the entire Mid-South likely warranting heat\nheadlines. Luckily, by Thursday, ensembles are hinting at lowered\nheights beginning to filter in from the Midwest as a secondary\nweak frontal boundary also begins to dip down. Confidence in\ntemperatures trending closer to normal by Friday and into the\nweekend continues to increase. Though subsequent Heat Advisories\nmay be warranted past Wednesday, oppressive heat is not expected.\nAs far as rain chances go, a cold frontal boundary and weak\ntroughing from the Midwest is expected to give lift to scattered\nshower and thunderstorm development both Thursday and Friday. If\nshower and thunderstorm chances pan out, we could be looking at a\nrelatively nice weekend with highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s.\n\nAEH",
